          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Fig. 9.8: Formation of an oxygen molecule"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 25,
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.687,
            0.888,
            0.738
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In this case, the two atoms are joined by two pairs of shared electrons, and are held together by a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "double bond",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". It can be depicted by drawing two lines between two oxygen atoms as O=O."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 26,
          "bbox": [
            0.225,
            0.744,
            0.487,
            0.762
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "B. Molecules of compounds"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 27,
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.766,
            0.89,
            0.836
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In the section 9.4.1A, you learnt about the formation of molecules of elements, where two atoms of the same element combine. What happens if atoms of two different elements combine? Let us understand by taking an example of the formation of hydrogen chloride molecule."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 28,
          "bbox": [
            0.225,
            0.841,
            0.601,
            0.859
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You can do it by using the following steps:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 29,
          "bbox": [
            0.232,
            0.861,
            0.888,
            0.896
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "y Write the electronic configurations of hydrogen and chlorine atoms (atomic number of hydrogen is 1 and chlorine is 17)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 30,
          "bbox": [
            0.232,
            0.898,
            0.889,
            0.933
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Calculate the number of electrons required by chlorine to complete its octet and by hydrogen to complete its duplet."
            }
          ]
        },
